--- mechanism: what `Neighbors` stores and what the port's classifier receives
  sklearn KNeighborsTransformer(n_neighbors=4): 5 entries per row, the cell itself included: row 0 -> [0, 1, 11, 9, 5], row 3 -> [3, 7, 9, 10, 5] (distances [0.0, 0.0, 0.421, 0.526, 0.647])
  Neighbors.compute_neighbors(4) stores 3 neighbours per row (self removed by *position*): row 0 -> [1, 11, 9], row 3 -> [7, 9, 10], row 7 -> [7, 9, 10]  <- row 7 keeps itself, its twin was in column 0
  classifier's _get_indices_distances_from_sparse_matrix(distances, 4): shape (12, 3); _has_self_column (an .any() over rows) = True -> no self column prepended, every cell gets 3 = k_adj-1 neighbours
  same without coincident points: shape (12, 4), rows with column 0 == the cell itself: 12 of 12 -> self column prepended, every cell gets k_adj-1 neighbours plus itself

--- the port's own pipeline (sc.pp.scrublet, exact neighbours): which rule applied, by seed
  seed 1, n_obs 1650: simulated parents drawn as (i,i) 0, unordered pair drawn twice 2 -> neighbour matrix (4950, 59) for k_adj = 60: k_adj-1 neighbours, no self
